N('potosi-mine-nv','Potosi lead-silver mine and camp','Las Vegas','nv','industry','ruins',35.96677,-115.54445,'area','ask','hike','','1856–1917',
  'Probably the oldest lode mine in Nevada. Mormon leader Brigham Young sent Nathaniel V. Jones to the "mountain of lead" in April 1856 and mining began on August 21 that year. Work went on in later booms, with a mining-gauge railway and cable lowering of ore built later on the cliff face.',
  'The 1974 Register form describes a large wooden structure with machinery at the mine portal, high on a cliff on Potosi Mountain, remains of the mine railway, and only foundations at the camp at Potosi Spring, 700 feet below. What stands today not checked.',
  'Nevada\'s oldest lode mine, a Mormon lead mine.',
  'Land manager and legal route not confirmed. The mine is high on a cliff and the hoist is hard to reach; old workings are dangerous. The land layer shows the Spring Mountains National Recreation Area (Forest Service); check with its office before going and treat the site as view-only until it confirms a route.',
  [['National Register record, Potosi','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/74001144']],brick='?'),
N('hidden-forest-cabin-nv','Hidden Forest Cabin','Las Vegas','nv','curio','standing',36.63173,-115.21893,'site','public','hike','','1890s?–1938',
  'A rough pine-log cabin in the Sheep Range, used over the years by bootleggers, prospectors and ranchers. The date it was built is unknown. Refuge staff have used it as an overnight shelter since 1938. The Register form notes much of its story is legend and unproven.',
  'A log cabin with a wood and tarpaper roof, two later windows, plank floor and stove. A search result quoting refuge material asks visitors not to enter it.',
  'A bootleggers\' cabin up a long refuge canyon.',
  'Desert National Wildlife Refuge (U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service). A route of about 5.5 miles on an old jeep road; the refuge gate opens about 4 a.m. and closes at sunset. Check the refuge page for road conditions and any permit. Leashed pets only. Do not go in the cabin.',
  [['National Register record, Hidden Forest Cabin','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/75001106'],['Discover the Hidden Forest Cabin, U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service','https://www.fws.gov/story/2022-03/discover-hidden-forest-cabin']],brick='no'),
N('bristol-wells-nv','Bristol Wells charcoal ovens and townsite','Pioche','nv','industry','ruins',38.10607,-114.69584,'area','public','drive','','1870–1918',
  'A silver-lead camp, first called National City and then Bristol City, with claims located in 1870 and a furnace in 1872. By 1882 it had stores, saloons, hotels and a newspaper. Ovens of shale and sandstone turned pine logs into charcoal for the smelter. When listed in 1972 three charcoal ovens, furnace residue and one original stone building remained.',
  'Beehive charcoal ovens, a stone house, a windmill, trough and corrals kept by cattlemen. What stands today not checked.',
  'Beehive ovens in a sagebrush basin.',
  'Reached by a graded dirt road west of U.S. 93 about 13 to 14 miles north of Pioche (sources differ on the exact mileage). The land layer shows Bureau of Land Management ground (Caliente Field Office); cattle gear on site is in use, so leave gates and troughs as found. Confirm road conditions with the BLM office.',
  [['National Register record, Bristol Wells Town Site','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/72000765'],['Bristol Well, Atlas Obscura','https://atlasobscura.com/places/bristol-well']],brick='no'),
